WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:04.266
PageMotor on a VPS, the easy way, with CloudPanel.

2
00:00:04.266 --> 00:00:09.920
One paste in the terminal to install the panel, and after that everything is clicks.

3
00:00:09.920 --> 00:00:12.881
About twenty minutes, and the panel is free.

4
00:00:13.581 --> 00:00:20.205
Here is why you would bother. The most common PageMotor bug is a design that will not apply:

5
00:00:20.205 --> 00:00:23.517
you save it, nothing changes, no error at all.

6
00:00:23.517 --> 00:00:30.356
Nine times out of ten that is a host refusing to let PHP write the compiled stylesheet to disk.

7
00:00:30.356 --> 00:00:40.795
On a CloudPanel VPS the problem is gone by construction: a real server, a writable filesystem, and site files owned by the same user PHP runs as.

8
00:00:41.495 --> 00:00:44.520
Two traps before you have even ordered.

9
00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:56.076
If your provider offers CloudPanel as a template, pick it inside the order wizard, under the Control panel tab, rather than a plain operating system.

10
00:00:56.076 --> 00:01:04.529
And when you create the site, choose PHP 8.3. The form may offer you something newer; 8.3 is the safe choice.

11
00:01:05.229 --> 00:01:08.616
Now the one to do before any site exists.

12
00:01:08.616 --> 00:01:18.362
CloudPanel images set the PHP timezone to Europe/Berlin in every PHP version they ship, whatever region the box is in,

13
00:01:18.362 --> 00:01:23.896
and PageMotor's browser sign-in fails on any timezone ahead of UTC.

14
00:01:23.896 --> 00:01:37.028
Set date.timezone to UTC in the PHP 8.3 FPM ini file and restart the pool first, because CloudPanel bakes the value into each site at the moment it is created.

15
00:01:37.028 --> 00:01:42.149
Forgot? It is editable per site, under Settings, PHP Settings.

16
00:01:42.849 --> 00:01:50.513
Installing the panel is three lines over SSH, and it is the only time you touch the command line.

17
00:01:50.513 --> 00:01:55.412
Five to ten minutes later, open your server's IP on port 8443,

18
00:01:55.412 --> 00:02:01.100
expect a certificate warning on a raw IP, and create your admin account.

19
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:06.013
Then the site and the database, both in the panel.

20
00:02:06.013 --> 00:02:16.209
Sites, Add Site, Create a PHP Site: generic PHP, version 8.3, your domain, and a site user whose password you write down.

21
00:02:16.209 --> 00:02:21.770
That site user is the user PHP runs as, which matters in a moment.

22
00:02:21.770 --> 00:02:32.218
Then Databases, Add Database. One rule: no underscores in the name or the user. CloudPanel simply rejects them. Use hyphens.

23
00:02:32.918 --> 00:02:41.673
Upload PageMotor into the document root using the panel's File Manager, or SFTP with the site-user login,

24
00:02:41.673 --> 00:02:47.759
and copy config-sample.php to config.php with your three database values.

25
00:02:47.759 --> 00:02:58.597
On current releases you can skip that and let the install wizard write the file. Either way, make /admin/ the first page you open.

26
00:02:58.597 --> 00:03:12.021
And the trap: extract the zip over SSH as root instead, and everything lands owned by root, PHP cannot save a thing, and it looks exactly like a PageMotor fault.

27
00:03:12.721 --> 00:03:20.527
PageMotor routes every request through index.php, so the vhost needs the front-controller rewrite.

28
00:03:20.527 --> 00:03:31.919
Current CloudPanel renders two server blocks per site: a front one on 80 and 443 that terminates TLS and proxies to a PHP backend on port 8080.

29
00:03:31.919 --> 00:03:39.885
The rewrite already ships in that backend block, so open the site's Vhost tab and check it is there.

30
00:03:39.885 --> 00:03:45.461
That one line is the difference between clean URLs and a wall of 404s.

31
00:03:46.161 --> 00:03:50.983
If you want to connect Claude, one thing on this stack needs your attention.

32
00:03:50.983 --> 00:04:00.058
CloudPanel's stock vhost lets /.well-known/ fall through to the filesystem for Let's Encrypt, which swallows the sign-in routes Claude follows.

33
00:04:00.058 --> 00:04:08.117
And because that block sits in the front server, where there is no PHP at all, the fix here is different from every other host:

34
00:04:08.117 --> 00:04:14.399
you must proxy_pass the rest of /.well-known/ to the site's 8080 backend, rather than try_files it.

35
00:04:14.399 --> 00:04:19.412
Apply it through the site's Vhost tab, so certificate renewal cannot revert it.

36
00:04:20.112 --> 00:04:25.711
One last thing that is easy to miss. Every mainstream VPS is sold self-managed,

37
00:04:25.711 --> 00:04:35.492
so from Ubuntu upwards it is yours: security patches, the panel, PHP, the database, the certificate. Budget about fifteen minutes a month.

38
00:04:35.492 --> 00:04:44.493
The guide has the exact vhost blocks, the Varnish and Cloudflare field notes, and a maintenance routine you can hand to Claude.

